All HTML elements can have attributes
Attributes provide additional information about elements
-> The href Attribute: <a href="https://www.w3schools.com">Visit W3Schools</a>
-> The src Attribute: <img src="img_girl.jpg">

There are two ways to specify the URL in the src attribute:
Absolute URL - Links to an external image that is hosted on another website.
Relative URL - Links to an image that is hosted within the website.

The width and height Attributes
<img src="img_girl.jpg" width="500" height="600">

-> The alt Attribute: <img src="img_girl.jpg" alt="Girl with a jacket">
The required alt attribute for the <img> tag specifies an alternate text for an image, if the image for some reason cannot be displayed.

-> The style Attribute: <p style="color:red;">This is a red paragraph.</p>
The style attribute is used to add styles to an element, such as color, font, size, and more.

-> The lang Attribute: <html lang="en">
You should always include the lang attribute inside the <html> tag, to declare the language of the Web page.

-> The title Attribute: <p title="I'm a tooltip">This is a paragraph.</p>
The title attribute defines some extra information about an element.

